<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="UTF-8"/><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width"/><meta name="description" content="Cycle racing in the 2010s: Team Sky, marginal gains, Ventoux, carbon, electronic shifting, nutrition and the biological passport."/><meta property="og:image" content="https://teob.dk/images/periods/2010-2019/wiggins-2012.webp"/><title>When the computer took over the bicycle · 2010–2019 · The Evolution of Bikes</title><link rel="stylesheet" href={`${base}/styles/global.css`}/></head><body><EnglishHeader current="periods" danishPath="/perioder/2010-2019/"/><main><article class="wildwest-page decade1970-page">
<header class="wildwest-hero decade1940-hero"><div><p class="section-label">The period 2010–2019</p><h1>When the computer took over<br/><em>the bicycle</em></h1></div><p>Carbon, electronic shifting and power measurement made the bicycle a precise instrument. Yet the decade’s strangest Tour image was a man running up Mont Ventoux in cycling shoes without his bicycle.</p></header><figure class="decade1970-hero-image"><img src={`${images}/wiggins-2012.webp`} alt="Bradley Wiggins in the peloton on the Champs-Élysées in 2012"/><figcaption>Wiggins protected in the peloton on the final stage. Photo: William Morice · CC BY 2.0.</figcaption></figure>
<section class="wildwest-lead decade1940-lead"><p class="section-label">Mont Ventoux · 2016</p><p>A camera motorcycle stopped in the crowd. Richie Porte and Bauke Mollema hit it, Chris Froome’s bicycle broke, and the yellow jersey began running up the mountain.</p></section><section class="era-facts decade1970-facts">{facts.map(([n,l])=><div><strong>{n}</strong><span>{l}</span></div>)}</section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">Team Sky</p><h2>Small gains organised into a system</h2></div><div><p>Team Sky made “marginal gains” the decade’s most repeated phrase: small improvements in training, recovery, aerodynamics, clothing and routines could add up. Wiggins won in 2012, Froome followed with four victories, and Geraint Thomas took over in 2018.</p><p>Later investigations into therapeutic exemptions and medical practices complicated the account. Data brought control, but not automatic trust.</p></div></section>
<section class="decade1970-mechanics"><div><p class="section-label">Carbon, electricity and hidden cables</p><h2>Weight reached the limit. Then air became the target.</h2><p>Carbon was standard in the professional peloton. With many bicycles below the UCI’s 6.8-kilogram minimum, development moved towards aerodynamics, stiffness and integration. Electronic systems became dependable, cables disappeared into frames and disc brakes began their slow arrival.</p></div><aside><span>Carbon</span><span>Di2 and EPS</span><span>Aero cockpit</span><span>Power meter</span><span>Disc brakes</span><span>6.8 kg</span></aside></section>
<section class="era-gallery"><figure class="gallery-tall"><img src={`${images}/froome-2016.webp`} alt="Chris Froome in the 2016 mountain time trial"/><figcaption>Froome in 2016. Photo: Konstantin Kleine · CC BY-SA 4.0.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/thomas-froome-2018.webp`} alt="Geraint Thomas and Chris Froome in 2018"/><figcaption>Thomas and Froome. Photo: filip bossuyt · CC BY 2.0.</figcaption></figure><figure><img src={`${images}/bernal-2019.webp`} alt="Egan Bernal in yellow in 2019"/><figcaption>Bernal in yellow. Photo: filip bossuyt · CC BY 2.0.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="decade1940-return"><div><span>Giro · 2014</span><h2>An attack amid unclear neutralisation</h2><p>Snow and cold caused confusion over whether the Stelvio descent was neutralised. Nairo Quintana continued with a group, won the stage and took the race lead.</p></div><div><span>Paris–Roubaix · 2016</span><h2>Garage fitness was enough</h2><p>Mathew Hayman broke his arm weeks before Roubaix and trained indoors. At 37, the domestique beat Tom Boonen on the velodrome.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy rust"><div><p class="section-label">Food by the gram</p><h2>The plan reached the rice cake</h2></div><div><p>Carbohydrate intake was calculated per hour and meals tailored to each stage and rider. The body remained a body: Tom Dumoulin stopped with acute stomach trouble in the 2017 Giro, limited his losses and later won. The often-repeated gelato explanation is not securely documented.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy"><div><p class="section-label">Control and old accounts</p><h2>The past did not vanish in 2013</h2></div><div><p>Lance Armstrong admitted doping in a 2013 television interview. The biological passport, targeted tests and stored samples strengthened control, but cases involving blood doping, weight-loss drugs and medical grey areas continued. Better surveillance was not proof that the problem had been solved.</p></div></section>
<section class="wildwest-copy dark"><div><p class="section-label">In the collection · 2013</p><h2>The Scapin K6 shows the new shape</h2></div><div><p>The collection’s Scapin K6 is a concrete example: a carbon frame with large shaped tubes and concealed solutions unavailable to the classic steel frame. Its form, finish, cable routing and components connect Italian identity with international production.</p><p><a href={`${base}/en/bikes/scapin-k6/`}>View the Scapin K6 →</a></p></div></section>
<section class="period-sources"><p class="section-label">Sources and notes</p><p>The gelato explanation is presented as anecdote. The Stelvio neutralisation was disputed during the race itself.</p></section><nav class="wildwest-next"><a href={`${base}/en/periods/2000-2009/`}>← 2000–2009</a><a href={`${base}/en/periods/`}>All periods →</a></nav></article></main><EnglishFooter/></body></html>
